WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:04.280
All right.

00:04.280 --> 00:09.200
So now we're going to talk about a very interesting operator function in SQL that exists.

00:09.200 --> 00:14.040
So now in some scenarios as you are querying the data from one table, you would need to go and check

00:14.040 --> 00:17.760
whether the rows of this table exist in another table.

00:17.760 --> 00:22.680
So that means you are checking like the existence of your rows in different table.

00:22.680 --> 00:28.160
And exactly in this scenario we go and use subqueries together with the operator exists.

00:28.160 --> 00:30.600
So the exist operator is very simple.

00:30.600 --> 00:35.360
It's just simply check whether the subquery returns any results any rows.

00:35.400 --> 00:35.680
All right.

00:35.720 --> 00:41.120
So now let's understand the syntax of the correlated subqueries using the exist operator.

00:41.160 --> 00:44.080
This can be a little bit complicated but we're going to do it step by step.

00:44.080 --> 00:45.240
Don't worry about it.

00:45.240 --> 00:47.200
So let's start with the easy stuff.

00:47.200 --> 00:47.960
And the main query.

00:48.000 --> 00:49.920
We're going to go and write a simple select.

00:49.920 --> 00:52.960
We are selecting few columns from the table two.

00:53.080 --> 00:55.400
And now we don't need all the data from table two.

00:55.440 --> 00:58.240
We want to filter the table using the Where clause.

00:58.280 --> 01:01.360
Now what we're going to do after the Where clause we're going to write immediately.

01:01.360 --> 01:03.560
Another keyword called exists.

01:03.560 --> 01:08.440
So we don't here specify any column before the exist like we have done in the comparison operator or

01:08.480 --> 01:09.480
the in operator.

01:09.480 --> 01:12.400
We don't need that because we are not filtering based on a value.

01:12.440 --> 01:14.400
We are filtering based on a logic.

01:14.400 --> 01:17.160
That's why we have the word exist immediately.

01:17.200 --> 01:21.400
And now directly after the exist we're going to go and define the subquery like this.

01:21.400 --> 01:24.680
So we're going to start saying select one from the table.

01:24.680 --> 01:25.320
Number one.

01:25.320 --> 01:27.600
Well it is not like a must or something.

01:27.600 --> 01:30.920
But it is very commonly used to specify here a one.

01:30.920 --> 01:35.120
We are not using the subquery in order to retrieve information from the table.

01:35.120 --> 01:41.200
One we are just testing whether the subquery is going to return a value or not, and we don't care about

01:41.200 --> 01:42.080
the returned value.

01:42.120 --> 01:42.960
It could be one.

01:42.960 --> 01:44.040
It could be column.

01:44.040 --> 01:45.320
It could be anything.

01:45.320 --> 01:48.640
So we don't care about the data that is retrieved.

01:48.680 --> 01:51.960
We are just care whether the subquery is returning anything.

01:51.960 --> 01:55.120
So that's why we go and write any value like here a one.

01:55.120 --> 01:56.600
So now we are not done yet.

01:56.720 --> 02:00.360
This subquery is not yet connected to the main query.

02:00.400 --> 02:02.720
We have somehow to go and connect them together.

02:02.720 --> 02:08.840
And we can do that using the Where clause, where we go and connect the ID from the table, one from

02:08.840 --> 02:13.600
the subquery with the ID from the outer query from the main query.

02:13.600 --> 02:19.520
And with that, we are building like a relationship between the subquery and the main query.

02:19.640 --> 02:24.800
So with that the subquery is now depending on the values from the main query.

02:24.800 --> 02:26.760
Because here we have the table two id.

02:26.800 --> 02:31.760
So the IDs from the main query gonna filter the subquery.

02:31.760 --> 02:38.560
So this is the syntax of correlated subqueries using the exists where we are making the subquery depending

02:38.600 --> 02:40.560
totally on the main query.

02:40.560 --> 02:42.800
So let's understand how exist works.

02:42.800 --> 02:49.120
So now for each row that we have from the main query it's gonna trigger and cause an execution of the

02:49.160 --> 02:50.040
subquery.

02:50.080 --> 02:52.960
This subquery can help us to evaluate this row.

02:52.960 --> 02:54.240
So we are testing this row.

02:54.280 --> 02:58.400
Now if the subquery doesn't return anything so there is no results.

02:58.400 --> 02:59.360
What can happen.

02:59.360 --> 03:05.410
The row that we are evaluating from the main query will be excluded from the final results.

03:05.410 --> 03:11.450
But now, in the other hand, if the subquery is returning a value so we have like some kind of results,

03:11.450 --> 03:16.610
then this row that we are evaluating is going to be included in the final results.

03:16.610 --> 03:19.690
So the subquery is used in order to do a test.

03:19.730 --> 03:21.570
Do we have a results or we don't.

03:21.610 --> 03:26.810
And based on this SQL either gonna include or exclude the row from the final results.

03:26.810 --> 03:30.090
So this is the logic behind the exists in SQL.

03:30.130 --> 03:30.410
All right.

03:30.410 --> 03:33.530
So now we're going to go and solve the same task using the exists.

03:33.730 --> 03:38.810
So the task says show the details of orders made by the customers in Germany.

03:38.810 --> 03:42.650
So we have already solved this task using the in operator and the subquery.

03:42.690 --> 03:45.170
Now we're going to go and solve it using the exists.

03:45.170 --> 03:48.410
So again we're going to have the same logical steps that we have done before.

03:48.730 --> 03:54.170
So first we're going to go and select all the details from the table sales orders.

03:54.330 --> 03:55.690
So let's execute it.

03:55.690 --> 03:58.770
And with that we have all the orders and all the details.

03:58.970 --> 04:01.130
But of course we don't need all those informations.

04:01.130 --> 04:05.090
We need only the orders that are made by customers from Germany.

04:05.090 --> 04:06.530
So that is the first query.

04:06.530 --> 04:08.930
Let's go and construct the second query.

04:08.970 --> 04:14.370
We're going to say select star from sales customers.

04:14.370 --> 04:15.690
But we don't need all the customers.

04:15.690 --> 04:21.650
We need only the customers from country equal to the value Germany.

04:21.850 --> 04:24.370
So let's go and execute it.

04:25.690 --> 04:28.570
So now we have all customers that come from Germany.

04:28.610 --> 04:33.650
Now we have to go and put those two queries together in order to get the final results.

04:33.690 --> 04:37.290
So as we learned before the second query is going to be our subquery.

04:37.290 --> 04:41.170
So it's going to be supporting the first query in order to filter the data.

04:41.250 --> 04:45.130
So the first query is going to be our main query.

04:45.170 --> 04:48.530
Let me just make this smaller and the text as well.

04:48.570 --> 04:50.890
Now we don't need all the orders right.

04:50.890 --> 04:54.210
We need only the orders where the customer come from Germany.

04:54.210 --> 04:55.890
So we need the Where clause.

04:55.930 --> 04:58.250
So now we can have the filter logic like this.

04:58.290 --> 05:04.090
Show the order details only if the customer ID exists from the subquery.

05:04.130 --> 05:06.650
And now we have to go and put our subquery.

05:06.650 --> 05:10.050
So our subquery is going to be this one over here?

05:10.810 --> 05:13.410
So let's just move it to the right side.

05:13.410 --> 05:17.290
And in order to have it as a subquery, we have to close that parenthesis.

05:17.330 --> 05:21.690
And now since exist is correlated subquery we cannot have it like this.

05:21.730 --> 05:25.890
We have to go and connect the subquery together with the main query.

05:25.890 --> 05:31.490
So now the subquery is currently independent from the main query because we want to check each order

05:31.490 --> 05:33.610
information from the order table.

05:33.650 --> 05:39.730
To check whether the customer exists in the subquery, we're going to go and add the condition like

05:39.730 --> 05:40.450
the following.

05:40.490 --> 05:42.370
And now it's like the joins.

05:42.370 --> 05:45.050
We have to go and connect the customer IDs together.

05:45.250 --> 05:47.770
So we're going to go over here and give it like an alias.

05:47.770 --> 05:49.570
And as well for the subquery.

05:49.610 --> 05:59.370
And now we're going to say customer ID from the orders should be equal to the customer ID from the subquery.

05:59.370 --> 06:01.210
The table customers like this.

06:01.210 --> 06:04.610
So again this customer ID come from the subquery.

06:04.610 --> 06:08.090
And this customer ID comes from the main query.

06:08.090 --> 06:14.050
So now since we are using the subquery only in order to test the existence of the customer.

06:14.050 --> 06:19.890
So if the subquery returns anything or not, it doesn't matter what you are selecting in the subquery.

06:19.890 --> 06:23.970
So so you can go with the star or a column or any static value.

06:23.970 --> 06:28.450
But for some reason all the SQL developers decided to go with the static value one.

06:28.450 --> 06:34.530
And of course you can go and add like a column like the customer ID, but it's like unnecessary step

06:34.530 --> 06:39.850
for the SQL in order to retrieve the information from the customer ID, so it can be way faster for

06:39.850 --> 06:40.250
SQL.

06:40.290 --> 06:41.810
If you say, okay, select one.

06:41.810 --> 06:43.930
So let's stick with the best practices.

06:43.930 --> 06:47.610
Use the one value if you are working with exist.

06:47.610 --> 06:51.930
So this is our subquery and I think we have everything.

06:51.930 --> 06:53.410
Let's go and execute it.

06:53.450 --> 06:58.810
Now as you can see in the output we got all the orders where the customers come from Germany.

06:58.850 --> 07:04.610
Now of course if you want to go and try another value and execute you will get exactly the same results.

07:04.610 --> 07:06.890
So it doesn't matter which value you are using.

07:06.890 --> 07:09.930
So with that, we have solved the task this time using the exists.

07:09.970 --> 07:15.540
Now if the task says is, show the details of orders made by customers that don't come from Germany.

07:15.580 --> 07:16.780
It's going to be very simple.

07:16.780 --> 07:20.580
We're going to go and use the operator, not before they exist.

07:20.580 --> 07:22.860
So where not exists.

07:22.860 --> 07:28.700
So now we are flipping the whole logic and we are saying there should be no matching with the subquery.

07:28.700 --> 07:35.380
So now if you go and execute it you will get all the orders where the customers don't come from Germany

07:35.380 --> 07:37.700
by simply using the not logic.

07:37.740 --> 07:41.060
And there is one more thing that is annoying about that correlated subqueries.

07:41.060 --> 07:44.380
If you compare it to the non correlated subqueries, as we learned before.

07:44.420 --> 07:46.780
Let me go back to the n operator.

07:46.820 --> 07:49.180
Now this is a non correlated subquery.

07:49.180 --> 07:54.260
And if I go and select only the subquery I can go and execute it independently.

07:54.740 --> 07:58.740
So I can go and check the intermediate results and like validate my query.

07:58.740 --> 08:04.660
But the problem with the correlated subquery I cannot go and highlight the subquery and then go and

08:04.660 --> 08:05.380
execute it.

08:05.380 --> 08:11.700
And that's because in the syntax of the subquery we are adding a column that is outside our subquery

08:11.700 --> 08:13.140
that come from the main query.

08:13.140 --> 08:17.540
So this piece of information currently for the SQL is unknown.

08:17.540 --> 08:18.940
And that's why we are getting this error.

08:18.940 --> 08:22.180
Because SQL saying okay I don't know where this column come from.

08:22.180 --> 08:23.620
So this is a little bit annoying.

08:23.620 --> 08:27.580
Using the correlated subqueries you cannot go and test the intermediate results.

08:27.580 --> 08:32.180
But how I usually do it, I go and test like an intermediate result for only one row.

08:32.180 --> 08:36.620
So for example I'm going to go and pick like a customer here for example two.

08:36.820 --> 08:41.620
So I'm going to go and say okay the customer ID should be equal to two.

08:41.660 --> 08:43.620
So let me just remove this from here.

08:43.620 --> 08:46.300
I got this value from the main query.

08:46.700 --> 08:49.940
So if I go now and execute it I can see here okay.

08:49.980 --> 08:54.260
The subquery is not returning anything because there is no such a value.

08:54.260 --> 08:56.860
So with that I'm just testing like one row.

08:56.900 --> 09:03.420
And of course in order to make this working I have to go and add as well the column from the main query.

09:03.420 --> 09:09.220
So this is why correlated subqueries are a little bit more hard to understand compared to the non-correlated,

09:09.220 --> 09:13.220
because we cannot go and test the intermediate results like we can do there.

09:13.220 --> 09:15.580
So this is another way on how to solve this task.

09:15.620 --> 09:19.540
Using a correlated subqueries with the operator exists.

09:19.580 --> 09:19.820
Okay.

09:19.860 --> 09:25.940
So now let's see step by step how SQL executed the correlated subqueries using the exists operator.

09:25.980 --> 09:29.220
So now this time SQL will not start with the subquery.

09:29.260 --> 09:32.900
It's going to go and start immediately with the main query SQL.

09:32.940 --> 09:35.220
First going to identify the main query.

09:35.220 --> 09:36.820
And it's going to go and execute it.

09:36.820 --> 09:38.740
But it's going to execute it row by row.

09:38.740 --> 09:41.940
So the first row is going to be the first customer.

09:41.940 --> 09:45.660
So now SQL can go and put the first customer under the test.

09:45.660 --> 09:51.940
So now the next step is that is going to go and pass the value of the customer ID from the main query

09:51.940 --> 09:53.140
to the subquery.

09:53.140 --> 09:55.300
So we are doing now exactly the opposite.

09:55.300 --> 09:59.860
So now what can happen is we're going to prepare the subquery with the following information.

09:59.860 --> 10:02.700
So we are saying the customer ID equal to one.

10:02.700 --> 10:05.300
And then SQL going to go and execute it.

10:05.300 --> 10:09.180
So now once the SQL executed this query we will get the result of one.

10:09.180 --> 10:14.380
And that's because we have here multiple times where the customer ID is equal to one.

10:14.380 --> 10:18.900
So there is rose in the order table where the customer ID equal to one.

10:18.900 --> 10:20.260
So now what can happen.

10:20.300 --> 10:23.380
The row from the main query gonna pass the test.

10:23.380 --> 10:26.860
And this customer can be included in the final results.

10:26.860 --> 10:31.380
So now the next step that is going to go and start testing the second customer.

10:31.380 --> 10:34.340
So we're going to put this customer under the test.

10:34.380 --> 10:37.180
Now it's going to go and pass the value to the subquery.

10:37.180 --> 10:38.900
So here we're going to have the value of two.

10:39.020 --> 10:41.260
And then SQL going to go and execute this query.

10:41.260 --> 10:45.940
And of course we will get a result because we have here multiple times where the customer ID equal to

10:45.980 --> 10:46.300
two.

10:46.540 --> 10:50.140
So that's why in the output of this subquery we will get one.

10:50.140 --> 10:53.460
So now it's going to say great we have a value from the subquery.

10:53.660 --> 10:57.580
That's why it is safe to show this customer in the outputs.

10:57.620 --> 11:00.660
And now it's going to go to the next row and so on.

11:00.660 --> 11:04.380
So for the next two customers the same thing's going to happen.

11:04.380 --> 11:07.780
All of those customers will have a value from the subquery.

11:07.780 --> 11:11.300
And that's why they are all like passing the test.

11:11.300 --> 11:13.020
So we will have it in the outputs.

11:13.060 --> 11:16.620
Now it's going to go to the last row from the table customers.

11:16.620 --> 11:20.350
So we have the Anna and we're going to put Anna to the test.

11:20.350 --> 11:24.950
So now what's going to happen is we're going to go and pass the value five to the subquery.

11:24.990 --> 11:28.470
And it's going to go and execute this query to the table orders.

11:28.510 --> 11:32.590
Now once SQL execute this query there will be nothing returned.

11:32.590 --> 11:38.110
And that's because we don't have here in the table orders a customer ID equal to five.

11:38.190 --> 11:42.110
And now SQL going to say, well we are not getting any results from the subquery.

11:42.350 --> 11:44.870
That's why this customer is going to fail.

11:45.070 --> 11:48.150
And SQL will not show it at the output.

11:48.150 --> 11:50.230
So it will be completely removed.

11:50.230 --> 11:54.870
So the customer Anna is excluded because the subquery is not returning anything.

11:54.910 --> 11:59.630
Customer ID number five Anna does not exist in the table orders.

11:59.630 --> 12:00.790
So it can fail the test.

12:00.790 --> 12:04.710
And we will have in the final results only four customers.

12:04.710 --> 12:06.910
So this is exactly the purpose of the exist.

12:06.950 --> 12:13.470
We are checking and testing the existence of our rows from another table from another query.

12:13.470 --> 12:19.190
So this is how SQL executes the correlated subqueries using the operator exists.
